<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Inn (river)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Inn is a river of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, flowing about 520 kilometres from the Swiss Engadine through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a>. Its valley, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innsbruck">Innsbruck</a> at its centre, was a main route through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, and the river long carried salt and other goods.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/inn-river.jpg" alt="Inn (river)" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wolfgang Glock · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:UnterinntalWest.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
